Scoped visibility experimental interface tools
==============================================

svctl is a small administrator-facing interface tool, not a startup
coordinator, daemon, Android policy generator, or complete privacy fix.
The kernel performs the final initial-user-namespace CAP_SYS_ADMIN check.
Use only with the matching experimental UAPI/kernel; ioctl magic 0xF7 is
a local experimental range, not an upstream allocation or stable ABI.

  svctl status
  svctl clear EXPECTED_GENERATION
  svctl replace EXPECTED_GENERATION CONFIG

status prints the generation, entry count, and each registered object's
device major/minor and inode number. Those identifiers are diagnostics,
not authorization tokens. Read the current generation, prepare a reviewed
complete snapshot, then explicitly pass that generation to replace/clear.
A competing update causes an error; svctl does not silently retry or adopt
a newer generation. clear publishes an empty snapshot.

CONFIG contains one directory path, a literal TAB, then comma-separated
inclusive decimal UID intervals per line. Example (replace <TAB> with a tab):

  /tmp/reviewed-private-container<TAB>0-0,1001-1001

Blank lines and lines beginning with # are ignored. Intervals must be
ascending, nonoverlapping, and within 0..4294967294 (4294967295 is an invalid
kernel UID). Signs, whitespace in numbers, trailing characters, malformed
intervals, more than 16 intervals, and more than 32 directories are rejected.
CRLF input is accepted. Duplicate directory objects are rejected by the
kernel. Empty CONFIG publishes an empty snapshot; inspect files before use.
Directory names cannot contain tabs/newlines. The final pathname component
must be a directory, not a symlink; intermediate pathname symlinks follow
normal Linux resolution. Directory opens use O_PATH|O_DIRECTORY|O_NOFOLLOW.

The control file is opened first; all acquired directory FDs and the control
FD close after completion or error. Kernel-held pins survive successful
publication until a subsequent replacement/clear. svctl never derives UIDs
from package names, automatically enrolls default paths, mounts directories,
starts services, or coordinates readiness/lifecycle. Existing descendant FDs,
recreated objects, deeper exports, native own/shared-UID isolation, and app
startup ordering require separate integration and acceptance testing.

scoped_visibility_test is the public-interface regression executable.
By default absent control skips with exit 4; --require-control fails with
exit 1 when control is absent. Successful generic interface checks are not
Android ordinary-App acceptance or proof of complete FUSE privacy repair.
